  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nAs of 2 AM Thursday morning, quasi-zonal midlevel flow remains \nacross the central Plains. Further north, a shortwave trough was was \nadvancing through southern Ontario with an attendant surface trough \nstalled from the OK Panhandle through central KS and northeast from \nthere. The forecast area has remained storm free so far with the \nstrongest forcing for ascent remaining across far northeast KS and \nnorthern MO. Midlevel heights will gradually increase through the \ndaytime hours today as shortwave ridging amplifies ahead of a \nwestern CONUS trough. Despite the increasing midlevel heights, a \nweak perturbation currently over northern NM is progged to lift \nacross the post-frontal region of west-central into central KS this \nafternoon and evening. This could support a few showers or \nthunderstorms. Steepening lapse rates may yield up to 1000 J/kg of \nelevated instability across central KS. Effective shear less than 30 \nkt should keep any convective disorganized. Otherwise, a notable \ntemperature gradient is expected to reside on the stalled surface \nfront this afternoon with highs across central KS in the upper 60s \nor lower 70s and areas of south-central and southeast KS near \n80.\n\nThe next shortwave trough is posed to lift from the Baja region late \ntonight into Friday. This should result in widespread rain showers \nFriday and even Saturday. SREF/EPS mean rainfall amounts Friday into \nSaturday are generally 0.50\" or less with the highest amounts along \nand west of I-135. \n\nTransitioning into the late Sunday into next timeframe, a strong \nmidlevel trough is progged to eject into the Plains Monday into \nMonday night. Confidence continues to increase for widespread \nrainfall beginning late Sunday night or Monday morning and \ncontinuing, at least intermittently, through Tuesday. Additionally, \nthe pattern, especially Monday afternoon and evening, may support a \nsevere storm potential and needs to be monitored over the coming \ndays. Stay tuned.",
